The '''''Caliciviridae''''' are a family of "small round structured" [[viruses]], members of Class IV of the [[Baltimore scheme]]. Caliciviridae bear resemblance to enlarged picornavirus and were formerly considered to be a genus within the Picornaviridae.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Caliciviridae - Caliciviridae - Positive-sense RNA Viruses - ICTV|url=https://ictv.global/report/chapter/caliciviridae/caliciviridae|access-date=2021-03-12|website=talk.ictvonline.org|language=en}}</ref> They have [[positive-sense]], single-stranded [[RNA]] which is not segmented.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Vinjé |first1=J |last2=Estes |first2=MK |last3=Esteves |first3=P |last4=Green |first4=KY |last5=Katayama |first5=K |last6=Knowles |first6=NJ |last7=L'Homme |first7=Y |last8=Martella |first8=V |last9=Vennema |first9=H |last10=White |first10=PA |last11=ICTV Report Consortium |title=ICTV Virus Taxonomy Profile: ''Caliciviridae''. |journal=The Journal of General Virology |date=November 2019 |volume=100 |issue=11 |pages=1469–1470 |doi=10.1099/jgv.0.001332 |pmid=31573467|pmc=7011698 |doi-access=free }}</ref> Thirteen species are placed in this family, divided among eleven genera.<ref name="ICTV">{{cite web |title=ICTV Report Caliciviridae |url=http://www.ictv.global/report/caliciviridae}}</ref> Diseases associated with the '''calicivirus''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|k|æ|l|ɪ|s|ɪ|ˌ|v|aɪ|r|ə|s|,_|k|ə|ˈ|l|ɪ|s|ɪ|ˌ|v|aɪ|r|ə|s}}) family include [[feline_calicivirus| feline calicivirus]], a respiratory disease, rabbit hemorrhagic disease virus, which causes a usually fatal hepatitis, and the Norwalk group of viruses, which cause gastroenteritis.<ref name=ICTV/><ref name=ViralZone>{{cite web|title=Viral Zone|url=http://viralzone.expasy.org/all_by_species/32.html|publisher=ExPASy|access-date=15 June 2015}}</ref> Caliciviruses naturally infect [[vertebrate]]s, and have been found in a number of organisms such as [[humans]], [[cattle]], [[pig]]s, [[Feline calicivirus|cats]], [[chicken]]s, [[reptile]]s, [[dolphin]]s and [[amphibian]]s. The caliciviruses have a simple construction and are not [[viral envelope|enveloped]]. The [[capsid]] appears [[hexagonal]]/[[spherical]] and has [[icosahedral]] symmetry (T=1<ref>[http://viralzone.expasy.org/all_by_protein/1057.html T=1]</ref> or T=3<ref>[http://viralzone.expasy.org/all_by_protein/806.html T=3]</ref><ref name=ViralZone />) with a diameter of 35–39&nbsp;nm.<ref name= HansmanGS>{{cite book |editor= Hansman, GS| year=2010 |title=Caliciviruses: Molecular and Cellular Virology| publisher=[[Caister Academic Press]] | isbn= 978-1-904455-63-9}}</ref>

Caliciviruses are not very well studied because until 2010, they could not be grown in [[cell culture|culture]], and they have a very narrow host range and no suitable animal model. However, the  application of modern genomic technologies has led to an increased understanding of the virus family.<ref name="HansmanGS"/> A 2013 isolate from [[rhesus monkey]]s—[[Tulane virus]]—can be grown in culture, and this system promises to increase understanding of these viruses.<ref name=Yu2013>Yu G, Zhang D, Guo F, Tan M, Jiang X, Jiang W (2013). "Cryo-EM structure of a novel calicivirus, Tulane Virus". ''PLoS One'' 8(3):e59817. {{doi|10.1371/journal.pone.0059817|doi-access=free}}</ref>

== Etymology ==
''Calici''- comes from the Latin word ''calix'', which means "cup" or "goblet". This refers to the cup-shaped depressions on the surface of the virions of viruses in the family. The suffix -''viridae'' is the suffix used for virus families.<ref>{{cite web |title=Taxon Details: ''Caliciviridae'' |url=https://ictv.global/taxonomy/taxondetails?taxnode_id=202402798&taxon_name=Caliciviridae |publisher=International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses |access-date=1 August 2025}}</ref>

